::: callout-note
## Output

This section imports and audits the organization chart, confirms John Windward's organizational role, and safely extracts selected fields from the nested event details.

The enriched event table will be used in the next phase to identify suspicious instruction-file families, reconstruct handoff chains, and compare earlier incidents with the latest SwiftWren event.
:::

# 6 Suspicious Instruction-File Propagation Chains

## 6.1 Validating the Extracted Instruction Files

The previous section identifies three suspicious instruction-file families:

- `HiddenOrca`
- `MellowOtter`
- `SwiftWren`

Each record represents a `queue_subordinate_task` event involving a `_further_instructions.md` file. These events are reviewed before reconstructing the propagation chains.

```{r}
#| label: validate-instruction-file-events

instruction_file_validation <- suspicious_instruction_events %>%
  count(
    file_family,
    file_path,
    short_name,
    detail_task,
    sort = TRUE
  )

instruction_file_validation
```

```{r}
#| label: validate-instruction-file-total

instruction_file_total <- suspicious_instruction_events %>%
  summarise(
    total_instruction_events = n(),
    total_file_families = n_distinct(
      file_family
    )
  )

instruction_file_total
```

The extracted records confirm that the suspicious instruction-file activity is concentrated in three file families. `SwiftWren` is the largest family, followed by `HiddenOrca` and `MellowOtter`.

## 6.2 Creating the Agent Extraction Function

Each suspicious handoff event contains a list of parties. The following function extracts personal-agent identifiers from each event.

The actor-normalisation function created earlier is reused so that inconsistent capitalisation or prefix formats do not create duplicated network nodes.

```{r}
#| label: define-agent-extraction-function

extract_agent_parties <- function(x) {
  agent_values <- as.character(
    x
  ) %>%
    keep(
      ~ str_detect(
        .x,
        regex(
          "^agent[:/]person:",
          ignore_case = TRUE
        )
      )
    ) %>%
    normalise_actor()

  unique(
    agent_values
  )
}
```

## 6.3 Extracting Source and Target Agents

The source agent is extracted from the first personal-agent identifier in the party list.

The target agent is primarily extracted from the nested `target_agent` field. If this field is unavailable, the second personal-agent identifier in the party list is used as a fallback.

```{r}
#| label: extract-suspicious-handoffs

suspicious_handoffs <- suspicious_instruction_events %>%
  mutate(
    agent_parties = map(
      parties,
      extract_agent_parties
    ),
    source_agent = map_chr(
      agent_parties,
      ~ dplyr::first(
        .x,
        default = NA_character_
      )
    ),
    target_agent_from_parties = map_chr(
      agent_parties,
      ~ dplyr::nth(
        .x,
        2,
        default = NA_character_
      )
    ),
    target_agent_from_details = normalise_actor(
      detail_target_agent
    ),
    target_agent = coalesce(
      target_agent_from_details,
      target_agent_from_parties
    ),
    source_label = format_actor_label(
      source_agent
    ),
    target_label = format_actor_label(
      target_agent
    ),
    self_loop = source_agent == target_agent
  ) %>%
  select(
    event_order,
    id,
    timestamp_local,
    short_name,
    file_family,
    file_path,
    source_agent,
    target_agent,
    source_label,
    target_label,
    self_loop,
    virus_flag
  ) %>%
  arrange(
    timestamp_local,
    event_order
  )

glimpse(
  suspicious_handoffs
)
```

## 6.4 Auditing Handoff Extraction Quality

The extracted handoff table is checked for duplicated event IDs, missing source agents, missing target agents, and self-loop handoffs.

A self-loop occurs when an agent delegates the suspicious task back to itself. These events may indicate repeated or inefficient task propagation.

```{r}
#| label: audit-suspicious-handoffs

handoff_quality_summary <- suspicious_handoffs %>%
  summarise(
    total_handoffs = n(),
    unique_event_ids = n_distinct(
      id
    ),
    duplicate_event_ids = n() - n_distinct(
      id
    ),
    missing_source_agents = sum(
      is.na(
        source_agent
      )
    ),
    missing_target_agents = sum(
      is.na(
        target_agent
      )
    ),
    self_loop_handoffs = sum(
      self_loop,
      na.rm = TRUE
    )
  )

handoff_quality_summary
```

The handoff-quality audit confirms that all suspicious propagation records contain valid source and target agents. No duplicated event IDs or missing agent identifiers are detected.

## 6.5 Reviewing Missing Source or Target Agents

If missing source or target identifiers exist, the affected records are displayed for review.

```{r}
#| label: inspect-missing-handoff-agents

missing_handoff_agents <- suspicious_handoffs %>%
  filter(
    is.na(
      source_agent
    ) |
      is.na(
        target_agent
      )
  )

missing_handoff_agents
```

No suspicious handoff records contain missing source or target agents.

## 6.6 Creating Suspicious-Chain Summary Statistics

The three suspicious file families are compared using:

- total number of handoffs;
- number of unique agents;
- number of self-loops;
- first observed handoff;
- last observed handoff;
- propagation duration.

```{r}
#| label: create-suspicious-chain-summary

suspicious_chain_summary <- suspicious_handoffs %>%
  group_by(
    file_family
  ) %>%
  summarise(
    handoff_count = n(),
    unique_agents = n_distinct(
      c(
        source_agent,
        target_agent
      ),
      na.rm = TRUE
    ),
    self_loop_count = sum(
      self_loop,
      na.rm = TRUE
    ),
    first_seen = min(
      timestamp_local,
      na.rm = TRUE
    ),
    last_seen = max(
      timestamp_local,
      na.rm = TRUE
    ),
    duration_hours = as.numeric(
      difftime(
        last_seen,
        first_seen,
        units = "hours"
      )
    ),
    .groups = "drop"
  ) %>%
  arrange(
    first_seen
  )

suspicious_chain_summary
```

The chain-level summary shows that `SwiftWren` is the dominant propagation chain. It contains substantially more handoffs than the earlier `HiddenOrca` and `MellowOtter` events.

## 6.7 Interactive Suspicious-Chain Summary

```{r}
#| label: display-suspicious-chain-summary

DT::datatable(
  suspicious_chain_summary %>%
    mutate(
      duration_hours = round(
        duration_hours,
        2
      )
    ),
  rownames = FALSE,
  options = list(
    dom = "t",
    scrollX = TRUE
  )
)
```

## 6.8 Creating a Repeated-Agent Summary

Some personal agents appear repeatedly across suspicious propagation chains.

The following table measures:

- the number of suspicious-chain participations;
- the number of distinct file families involving each agent;
- the number of appearances as a source agent;
- the number of appearances as a target agent.

```{r}
#| label: create-repeated-agent-summary

source_agent_summary <- suspicious_handoffs %>%
  transmute(
    file_family,
    agent_id = source_agent,
    agent_label = source_label,
    role = "Source"
  )

target_agent_summary <- suspicious_handoffs %>%
  transmute(
    file_family,
    agent_id = target_agent,
    agent_label = target_label,
    role = "Target"
  )

repeated_agent_summary <- bind_rows(
  source_agent_summary,
  target_agent_summary
) %>%
  filter(
    !is.na(
      agent_id
    )
  ) %>%
  group_by(
    agent_id,
    agent_label
  ) %>%
  summarise(
    suspicious_participations = n(),
    number_of_file_families = n_distinct(
      file_family
    ),
    source_appearances = sum(
      role == "Source"
    ),
    target_appearances = sum(
      role == "Target"
    ),
    .groups = "drop"
  ) %>%
  arrange(
    desc(
      number_of_file_families
    ),
    desc(
      suspicious_participations
    )
  )

repeated_agent_summary
```

The most active suspicious-chain participants are not necessarily the final decision points. For example, John Windward Agent may appear less frequently than several other agents, but its role remains operationally important because the anomalous SaidIT post was ultimately associated with John Windward's identity.

## 6.9 Interactive Repeated-Agent Table

