::: callout-warning
## Key Incident Evidence

The final SwiftWren handoffs reveal a clear propagation path immediately before the anomalous SaidIT post:

`Daniel Gangway Agent → Chloe Ballast Agent → John Windward Agent`

The event log also confirms that:

- the final delegated task was linked to `SwiftWren_further_instructions.md`;
- the public SaidIT post used `SwiftWren.txt` as its content source;
- `SwiftWren_further_instructions.md` and `SwiftWren.txt` were deleted immediately after the post.

These records support the conclusion that the anomalous public release was the visible endpoint of a longer instruction-file propagation chain rather than an isolated posting error.
:::

::: callout-note
## Part 5 Output

This section reconstructs suspicious instruction-file handoffs at the personal-agent level.

Three recurring file families are identified: `HiddenOrca`, `MellowOtter`, and `SwiftWren`. The extracted handoff table records the source agent, target agent, timestamp, file family, and self-loop status for each propagation event.

The handoff-quality audit confirms that the extracted records are structurally complete. The latest `SwiftWren` chain is isolated for detailed review because it contains substantially more propagation events than the earlier chains.

The final posting window will be used in the next section to reconstruct the exact sequence that led to the anomalous SaidIT post.
:::

# 7 Historical Chain Comparison and Visual Analytics

## 7.1 Loading Network-Analysis Packages

The previous sections identify three recurring suspicious instruction-file families and reconstruct their source-target handoffs.

This section uses visual analytics to compare the historical chains, identify repeatedly involved agents, and reconstruct the latest `SwiftWren` incident. Network-analysis packages are loaded separately because they are only required after the handoff table has been created.

```{r}
#| label: load-network-packages

pacman::p_load(
  igraph,
  tidygraph,
  ggraph,
  visNetwork,
  graphlayouts,
  ggrepel
)
```

## 7.2 Preparing the Historical Comparison Table

The three suspicious chains are compared across four indicators:

- handoff count;
- number of unique agents;
- self-loop count;
- propagation duration in hours.

The comparison table is converted into a long format to support faceted visualisation.

```{r}
#| label: create-historical-comparison-table

historical_comparison_long <- suspicious_chain_summary %>%
  select(
    file_family,
    handoff_count,
    unique_agents,
    self_loop_count,
    duration_hours
  ) %>%
  pivot_longer(
    cols = c(
      handoff_count,
      unique_agents,
      self_loop_count,
      duration_hours
    ),
    names_to = "metric",
    values_to = "value"
  ) %>%
  mutate(
    metric = recode(
      metric,
      "handoff_count" = "Handoff Count",
      "unique_agents" = "Unique Agents",
      "self_loop_count" = "Self-Loops",
      "duration_hours" = "Duration in Hours"
    ),
    file_family = factor(
      file_family,
      levels = c(
        "HiddenOrca",
        "MellowOtter",
        "SwiftWren"
      )
    ),
    latest_incident = file_family == "SwiftWren"
  )

historical_comparison_long
```

## 7.3 Comparing Historical Suspicious Chains

The faceted bar chart compares the three suspicious instruction-file families.

`SwiftWren` is highlighted because it is the latest and largest propagation chain.

```{r}
#| label: plot-historical-chain-comparison
#| fig-width: 10
#| fig-height: 7

ggplot(
  historical_comparison_long,
  aes(
    x = file_family,
    y = value,
    fill = latest_incident
  )
) +
  geom_col(
    width = 0.68,
    show.legend = FALSE
  ) +
  geom_text(
    aes(
      label = case_when(
        metric == "Duration in Hours" ~
          number(
            value,
            accuracy = 0.1
          ),
        TRUE ~
          number(
            value,
            accuracy = 1
          )
      )
    ),
    vjust = -0.35,
    size = 3.7
  ) +
  facet_wrap(
    ~ metric,
    scales = "free_y",
    ncol = 2
  ) +
  scale_fill_manual(
    values = c(
      "TRUE" = "#E76F51",
      "FALSE" = "#5DADE2"
    )
  ) +
  scale_y_continuous(
    expand = expansion(
      mult = c(
        0,
        0.15
      )
    )
  ) +
  labs(
    title = "Historical Comparison of Suspicious Instruction-File Chains",
    subtitle = "SwiftWren is the most extensive propagation chain across multiple indicators",
    x = NULL,
    y = NULL
  ) +
  theme_minimal(
    base_size = 13
  ) +
  theme(
    strip.text = element_text(
      face = "bold"
    ),
    plot.title = element_text(
      face = "bold"
    )
  )
```

The comparison chart shows that `SwiftWren` is materially larger and more persistent than the earlier `HiddenOrca` and `MellowOtter` chains. It contains the highest number of handoffs, involves the largest number of agents, and produces the most self-loop events.

## 7.4 Creating the Suspicious-Chain Timeline

A historical timeline is created to show when each handoff occurred.

Each point represents one suspicious instruction-file handoff. The final public incident time is marked with a vertical dashed line.

```{r}
#| label: create-suspicious-chain-timeline

suspicious_chain_timeline <- suspicious_handoffs %>%
  mutate(
    file_family = factor(
      file_family,
      levels = c(
        "HiddenOrca",
        "MellowOtter",
        "SwiftWren"
      )
    )
  )

suspicious_chain_timeline
```

## 7.5 Visualising the Suspicious-Chain Timeline

```{r}
#| label: plot-suspicious-chain-timeline
#| fig-width: 11
#| fig-height: 5.8

ggplot(
  suspicious_chain_timeline,
  aes(
    x = timestamp_local,
    y = file_family,
    color = file_family
  )
) +
  geom_point(
    alpha = 0.72,
    size = 2
  ) +
  geom_vline(
    xintercept = incident_time_local,
    linetype = "dashed",
    linewidth = 0.8,
    color = "#E76F51"
  ) +
  annotate(
    "text",
    x = incident_time_local - hours(4),
    y = 3.32,
    label = "Anomalous SaidIT post",
    color = "#E76F51",
    size = 3.7
  ) +
  scale_color_manual(
    values = c(
      "HiddenOrca" = "#5DADE2",
      "MellowOtter" = "#58D68D",
      "SwiftWren" = "#E76F51"
    )
  ) +
  scale_x_datetime(
    date_labels = "%b %d\n%H:%M",
    date_breaks = "1 day",
    expand = expansion(
      mult = c(
        0.02,
        0.05
      )
    )
  ) +
  labs(
    title = "Timeline of Suspicious Instruction-File Handoffs",
    subtitle = "Each point represents one agent-to-agent handoff",
    x = "Local Time",
    y = "File Family",
    color = "File Family"
  ) +
  theme_minimal(
    base_size = 13
  ) +
  theme(
    legend.position = "bottom",
    plot.title = element_text(
      face = "bold"
    )
  )
```

The historical timeline reveals that suspicious handoff activity is not limited to a single isolated event. Similar propagation patterns appear across multiple file families. The `SwiftWren` chain persists for the longest period and continues until shortly before the anomalous SaidIT post.

## 7.6 Preparing the Repeated-Agent Visualisation

The most frequently involved agents are selected for visual comparison.

An agent may appear as a source, a target, or both. The number of file families indicates whether the actor participates in only one chain or repeatedly appears across multiple suspicious chains.

```{r}
#| label: create-top-repeated-agents

top_repeated_agents <- repeated_agent_summary %>%
  slice_max(
    order_by = suspicious_participations,
    n = 15,
    with_ties = FALSE
  ) %>%
  mutate(
    agent_label = fct_reorder(
      agent_label,
      suspicious_participations
    ),
    number_of_file_families = factor(
      number_of_file_families
    )
  )

top_repeated_agents
```

## 7.7 Visualising Repeated Agent Participation

```{r}
#| label: plot-repeated-agent-participation
#| fig-width: 10
#| fig-height: 6.5

ggplot(
  top_repeated_agents,
  aes(
    x = suspicious_participations,
    y = agent_label,
    fill = number_of_file_families
  )
) +
  geom_col(
    width = 0.72
  ) +
  geom_text(
    aes(
      label = suspicious_participations
    ),
    hjust = -0.25,
    size = 3.6
  ) +
  scale_x_continuous(
    expand = expansion(
      mult = c(
        0,
        0.12
      )
    )
  ) +
  scale_fill_brewer(
    palette = "Blues",
    name = "Number of\nFile Families"
  ) +
  labs(
    title = "Agents Repeatedly Involved in Suspicious Handoffs",
    subtitle = "Frequent participation indicates exposure to recurring propagation chains",
    x = "Suspicious-Chain Participations",
    y = NULL
  ) +
  theme_minimal(
    base_size = 13
  ) +
  theme(
    legend.position = "right",
    plot.title = element_text(
      face = "bold"
    )
  )
```

The most frequently involved agents are not necessarily the final decision points. Some agents appear repeatedly because they act as central routing nodes. John Windward Agent appears less frequently than several other agents, but its role is still operationally important because it is linked to the final public posting stage.

## 7.8 Aggregating SwiftWren Network Edges

The `SwiftWren` chain is converted into an aggregated directed network.

Repeated handoffs between the same source and target agents are combined. An additional final edge is created to represent the public SaidIT posting endpoint confirmed in the final posting window.

```{r}
#| label: create-swiftwren-network-edges

swiftwren_network_edges <- swiftwren_handoffs %>%
  count(
    source_agent,
    target_agent,
    source_label,
    target_label,
    name = "handoff_count"
  ) %>%
  mutate(
    edge_type = "Agent Handoff"
  )

swiftwren_public_edge <- tibble(
  source_agent = "agent:person:john_windward",
  target_agent = "system:saidit",
  source_label = "John Windward Agent",
  target_label = "SaidIT",
  handoff_count = 1L,
  edge_type = "Public Post"
)

swiftwren_network_edges <- bind_rows(
  swiftwren_network_edges,
  swiftwren_public_edge
)

swiftwren_network_edges
```

## 7.9 Creating SwiftWren Network Nodes

The network-node table distinguishes ordinary agents, selected critical agents, and the external SaidIT endpoint.

```{r}
#| label: create-swiftwren-network-nodes

swiftwren_network_nodes <- bind_rows(
  swiftwren_network_edges %>%
    transmute(
      node_id = source_agent,
      node_label = source_label
    ),
  swiftwren_network_edges %>%
    transmute(
      node_id = target_agent,
      node_label = target_label
    )
) %>%
  distinct(
    node_id,
    .keep_all = TRUE
  ) %>%
  mutate(
    node_role = case_when(
      node_id == "system:saidit" ~
        "External Platform",
      node_id %in% c(
        "agent:person:john_windward",
        "agent:person:chloe_ballast",
        "agent:person:daniel_gangway"
      ) ~
        "Critical Agent",
      TRUE ~
        "Other Agent"
    ),
    display_label = case_when(
      node_id %in% c(
        "agent:person:john_windward",
        "agent:person:chloe_ballast",
        "agent:person:daniel_gangway",
        "system:saidit",
        "agent:person:emma_harbor"
      ) ~
        node_label,
      TRUE ~
        NA_character_
    )
  )

swiftwren_network_nodes
```

## 7.10 Creating the SwiftWren Directed Graph

The static graph object is created using `tidygraph`.

Degree and betweenness centrality are calculated to support later interpretation.

```{r}
#| label: create-swiftwren-directed-graph

swiftwren_graph <- tbl_graph(
  nodes = swiftwren_network_nodes,
  edges = swiftwren_network_edges %>%
    select(
      source_agent,
      target_agent,
      handoff_count,
      edge_type
    ),
  directed = TRUE
) %>%
  activate(
    nodes
  ) %>%
  mutate(
    in_degree = centrality_degree(
      mode = "in"
    ),
    out_degree = centrality_degree(
      mode = "out"
    ),
    total_degree = centrality_degree(
      mode = "all"
    ),
    betweenness = centrality_betweenness(
      directed = TRUE
    )
  )

swiftwren_graph
```

## 7.11 Visualising the SwiftWren Propagation Network

The static network shows the main structure of the `SwiftWren` chain.

Node size represents the total number of direct network connections. Critical agents and the external SaidIT endpoint are highlighted.

```{r}
#| label: plot-swiftwren-propagation-network
#| fig-width: 11
#| fig-height: 7.5

set.seed(
  1234
)

ggraph(
  swiftwren_graph,
  layout = "stress"
) +
  geom_edge_link(
    aes(
      width = handoff_count,
      color = edge_type
    ),
    alpha = 0.32,
    arrow = grid::arrow(
      length = grid::unit(
        2.2,
        "mm"
      ),
      type = "closed"
    ),
    end_cap = circle(
      2.6,
      "mm"
    ),
    show.legend = TRUE
  ) +
  geom_node_point(
    aes(
      size = total_degree,
      color = node_role
    ),
    alpha = 0.95
  ) +
  geom_node_text(
    aes(
      label = display_label
    ),
    repel = TRUE,
    size = 3.6,
    fontface = "bold",
    na.rm = TRUE
  ) +
  scale_edge_width(
    range = c(
      0.25,
      1.8
    )
  ) +
  scale_edge_color_manual(
    values = c(
      "Agent Handoff" = "#AAB7B8",
      "Public Post" = "#E76F51"
    )
  ) +
  scale_color_manual(
    values = c(
      "Other Agent" = "#85C1E9",
      "Critical Agent" = "#F5B041",
      "External Platform" = "#E76F51"
    )
  ) +
  scale_size_continuous(
    range = c(
      3,
      9
    )
  ) +
  labs(
    title = "SwiftWren Agent-to-Agent Propagation Network",
    subtitle = "Selected labels highlight the final path leading to the SaidIT endpoint",
    size = "Total Degree",
    color = "Node Role",
    edge_color = "Edge Type",
    edge_width = "Handoff Count"
  ) +
  theme_graph(
    base_family = "sans"
  ) +
  theme(
    legend.position = "right"
  )
```

The SwiftWren propagation network shows that the anomalous public post was not an isolated action. The suspicious instruction file circulated through a dense agent-to-agent network before reaching John Windward Agent. The highlighted path shows that Daniel Gangway Agent and Chloe Ballast Agent were involved shortly before the final handoff to John Windward Agent. The final public-post edge then connects John Windward Agent to SaidIT.

## 7.12 Creating the Interactive SwiftWren Network

The interactive network allows users to inspect individual actors and handoff relationships.

Users can search for a node, select an actor, and inspect neighbouring connections.

```{r}
#| label: prepare-interactive-swiftwren-network

swiftwren_vis_nodes <- swiftwren_graph %>%
  activate(
    nodes
  ) %>%
  as_tibble() %>%
  transmute(
    id = node_id,
    label = node_label,
    group = node_role,
    value = total_degree + 1,
    title = paste0(
      "<b>",
      node_label,
      "</b><br>",
      "Role: ",
      node_role,
      "<br>",
      "In-degree: ",
      in_degree,
      "<br>",
      "Out-degree: ",
      out_degree,
      "<br>",
      "Betweenness: ",
      round(
        betweenness,
        2
      )
    )
  )

swiftwren_vis_edges <- swiftwren_network_edges %>%
  transmute(
    from = source_agent,
    to = target_agent,
    value = handoff_count,
    arrows = "to",
    title = paste0(
      edge_type,
      "<br>",
      "Handoff Count: ",
      handoff_count
    )
  )

glimpse(
  swiftwren_vis_nodes
)

glimpse(
  swiftwren_vis_edges
)
```

```{r}
#| label: display-interactive-swiftwren-network

visNetwork(
  swiftwren_vis_nodes,
  swiftwren_vis_edges,
  height = "700px",
  width = "100%"
) %>%
  visNodes(
    shape = "dot",
    font = list(
      size = 16
    ),
    scaling = list(
      min = 10,
      max = 32
    )
  ) %>%
  visEdges(
    smooth = list(
      enabled = TRUE,
      type = "dynamic"
    ),
    scaling = list(
      min = 1,
      max = 5
    ),
    arrows = list(
      to = list(
        enabled = TRUE,
        scaleFactor = 0.55
      )
    ),
    color = list(
      opacity = 0.45
    )
  ) %>%
  visOptions(
    highlightNearest = list(
      enabled = TRUE,
      degree = 1,
      hover = TRUE
    ),
    nodesIdSelection = TRUE,
    selectedBy = "group"
  ) %>%
  visPhysics(
    solver = "barnesHut",
    stabilization = list(
      enabled = TRUE,
      iterations = 1200
    ),
    barnesHut = list(
      gravitationalConstant = -5000,
      centralGravity = 0.2,
      springLength = 170,
      springConstant = 0.04,
      damping = 0.25,
      avoidOverlap = 0.4
    )
  )
```

## 7.13 Creating the Final Incident Timeline

The final incident timeline focuses on the actions most directly related to public release.

The relevant events include:

- the final `SwiftWren` instruction handoff;
- the SaidIT pre-post check;
- the public SaidIT post;
- deletion of the related instruction and content files.

```{r}
#| label: create-final-incident-timeline

final_incident_timeline <- events_enriched %>%
  filter(
    timestamp_local >= incident_time_local - minutes(
      10
    ),
    timestamp_local <= incident_time_local + minutes(
      5
    )
  ) %>%
  mutate(
    event_group = case_when(
      short_name == "queue_subordinate_task" &
        str_detect(
          coalesce(
            file_path,
            ""
          ),
          "SwiftWren_further_instructions\\.md"
        ) ~
        "Instruction Handoff",
      short_name == "saidit_post_check" ~
        "Pre-Post Check",
      short_name == "saidit_post" ~
        "Public SaidIT Post",
      short_name == "delete_file" &
        detail_target %in% c(
          "SwiftWren_further_instructions.md",
          "SwiftWren.txt"
        ) ~
        "File Deletion",
      TRUE ~
        "Other Activity"
    ),
    event_description = case_when(
      timestamp_local == ymd_hms(
        "2046-05-17 04:12:24",
        tz = "America/Los_Angeles"
      ) ~
        "Daniel Gangway → Chloe Ballast",
      timestamp_local == ymd_hms(
        "2046-05-17 04:21:13",
        tz = "America/Los_Angeles"
      ) ~
        "Chloe Ballast → John Windward",
      event_group == "Pre-Post Check" ~
        "SaidIT pre-post check",
      event_group == "Public SaidIT Post" ~
        "Published SwiftWren.txt",
      event_group == "File Deletion" &
        detail_target == "SwiftWren_further_instructions.md" ~
        "Deleted instruction file",
      event_group == "File Deletion" &
        detail_target == "SwiftWren.txt" ~
        "Deleted content file",
      TRUE ~
        short_name
    )
  ) %>%
  filter(
    event_group != "Other Activity"
  ) %>%
  select(
    timestamp_local,
    short_name,
    event_group,
    event_description
  ) %>%
  arrange(
    timestamp_local
  )

final_incident_timeline
```

## 7.14 Visualising the Final Incident Timeline

```{r}
#| label: plot-final-incident-timeline
#| fig-width: 11
#| fig-height: 6.2

ggplot(
  final_incident_timeline,
  aes(
    x = timestamp_local,
    y = fct_rev(
      factor(
        event_group,
        levels = c(
          "Instruction Handoff",
          "Pre-Post Check",
          "Public SaidIT Post",
          "File Deletion"
        )
      )
    ),
    color = event_group
  )
) +
  geom_point(
    size = 4
  ) +
  geom_label_repel(
    aes(
      label = event_description
    ),
    size = 3.4,
    box.padding = 0.55,
    point.padding = 0.35,
    min.segment.length = 0,
    show.legend = FALSE,
    seed = 1234
  ) +
  scale_color_manual(
    values = c(
      "Instruction Handoff" = "#5DADE2",
      "Pre-Post Check" = "#F5B041",
      "Public SaidIT Post" = "#E76F51",
      "File Deletion" = "#AF7AC5"
    )
  ) +
  scale_x_datetime(
    date_labels = "%H:%M:%S",
    expand = expansion(
      mult = c(
        0.04,
        0.1
      )
    )
  ) +
  labs(
    title = "Final Sequence Before and After the Anomalous SaidIT Post",
    subtitle = "Two final handoffs are followed by a pre-post check, public release, and immediate file deletion",
    x = "Local Time on May 17, 2046",
    y = NULL,
    color = "Event Type"
  ) +
  theme_minimal(
    base_size = 13
  ) +
  theme(
    legend.position = "bottom",
    plot.title = element_text(
      face = "bold"
    )
  )
```

The final incident timeline reconstructs the exact sequence immediately before and after the anomalous SaidIT post. At 04:12:24, Daniel Gangway Agent delegated the suspicious instruction to Chloe Ballast Agent. At 04:21:13, Chloe Ballast Agent passed the instruction to John Windward Agent. One second later, the system performed a SaidIT pre-post check. The public post using `SwiftWren.txt` was released at 04:21:15. The related instruction file and content file were then deleted within the next two seconds.

## 7.15 Interactive Final Incident Table

The final event sequence is displayed as a compact interactive table.

```{r}
#| label: display-final-incident-timeline

DT::datatable(
  final_incident_timeline,
  rownames = FALSE,
  options = list(
    dom = "t",
    scrollX = TRUE
  )
)
```

::: callout-warning
## Key Visual Analytics Findings

The visual evidence identifies a recurring suspicious instruction-file propagation pattern.

- `HiddenOrca`, `MellowOtter`, and `SwiftWren` exhibit similar handoff behaviour.
- `SwiftWren` is the largest and most persistent chain.
- The latest chain reaches John Windward Agent shortly before the public SaidIT post.
- The post uses `SwiftWren.txt` as its confirmed content source.
- The related instruction and content files are deleted immediately after release.

The available evidence links the public release to John Windward Agent. The logs do not establish that John Windward personally authored or manually submitted the anomalous content.
:::

# 8 Content Origin Assessment and Historical Incident Evidence

## 8.1 Investigation Scope

The previous section reconstructs the propagation chain that led to the anomalous SaidIT post. This section focuses on the origin of the public-post content and examines whether similar public-release patterns occurred historically.

The analysis distinguishes between two forms of evidence:

- **confirmed evidence**, which is directly recorded in the event log;
- **remaining uncertainty**, which cannot be resolved because the full text contents of the suspicious files may not be available in the dataset.

This distinction is important because the event log can establish the operational pathway of the post without necessarily revealing the complete semantic meaning of the underlying text.

## 8.2 Defining Suspicious File Families

The three suspicious file families identified earlier are stored as a reusable vector.

```{r}
#| label: define-suspicious-file-families

suspicious_file_families <- suspicious_chain_summary %>%
  pull(
    file_family
  ) %>%
  unique()

suspicious_file_families
```

## 8.3 Reviewing SaidIT Posting Events

The event log is filtered to identify all SaidIT posting events.

Selected nested fields are extracted to determine:

- the posting timestamp;
- the actors involved;
- the forum destination;
- the content source;
- whether the content source belongs to a suspicious file family.

```{r}
#| label: extract-saidit-post-events

saidit_post_events <- events_enriched %>%
  filter(
    short_name == "saidit_post"
  ) %>%
  mutate(
    parties_display = map_chr(
      parties,
      ~ paste(
        .x,
        collapse = ", "
      )
    ),
    detail_forum = map_chr(
      details,
      ~ pluck_chr(
        .x,
        "forum"
      )
    ),
    content_family = str_remove(
      detail_content_source,
      "\\.txt$"
    ),
    suspicious_content_source = content_family %in%
      suspicious_file_families
  ) %>%
  select(
    event_order,
    id,
    timestamp_local,
    parties_display,
    detail_forum,
    detail_content_source,
    content_family,
    suspicious_content_source
  ) %>%
  arrange(
    timestamp_local,
    event_order
  )

saidit_post_events
```

## 8.4 Isolating Suspicious SaidIT Posts

The suspicious public-post records are isolated by matching each content source to the three suspicious file families.

```{r}
#| label: isolate-suspicious-saidit-posts

suspicious_saidit_posts <- saidit_post_events %>%
  filter(
    suspicious_content_source
  )

suspicious_saidit_posts
```

Three suspicious SaidIT posts are identified. All three posts were submitted by John Windward Agent to the general forum. Their content sources were `HiddenOrca.txt`, `MellowOtter.txt`, and `SwiftWren.txt`.

## 8.5 Interactive Suspicious SaidIT Post Table

```{r}
#| label: display-suspicious-saidit-posts

DT::datatable(
  suspicious_saidit_posts,
  rownames = FALSE,
  options = list(
    dom = "t",
    scrollX = TRUE
  )
)
```

The suspicious public-post records provide direct evidence of the content source used in each release. The `SwiftWren` incident is linked to `SwiftWren.txt`.

## 8.6 Inspecting the Available SaidIT Detail Fields

The nested structure of SaidIT posting events is reviewed to determine whether the full post text is available in the event log.

```{r}
#| label: inspect-saidit-detail-fields

saidit_detail_fields <- events_enriched %>%
  filter(
    short_name == "saidit_post"
  ) %>%
  transmute(
    id,
    timestamp_local,
    detail_fields = map(
      details,
      names
    )
  ) %>%
  unnest_longer(
    detail_fields,
    values_to = "detail_field"
  ) %>%
  count(
    detail_field,
    sort = TRUE
  )

saidit_detail_fields
```

## 8.7 Extracting Potential Text Fields

The following code checks several possible text fields that may exist in the nested SaidIT records.

If all extracted values are missing, the report should state that the dataset records the source filename but does not preserve the full public-post text.

```{r}
#| label: inspect-potential-post-text

saidit_post_text_check <- events_enriched %>%
  filter(
    short_name == "saidit_post"
  ) %>%
  mutate(
    parties_display = map_chr(
      parties,
      ~ paste(
        .x,
        collapse = ", "
      )
    ),
    possible_content = map_chr(
      details,
      ~ first_non_missing_chr(
        pluck_chr(
          .x,
          "content"
        ),
        pluck_chr(
          .x,
          "text"
        ),
        pluck_chr(
          .x,
          "message"
        ),
        pluck_chr(
          .x,
          "body"
        ),
        pluck_chr(
          .x,
          "post"
        )
      )
    )
  ) %>%
  select(
    id,
    timestamp_local,
    parties_display,
    detail_content_source,
    possible_content
  ) %>%
  arrange(
    timestamp_local
  )

saidit_post_text_check
```

## 8.8 Checking Whether Full Text Is Available

```{r}
#| label: summarise-post-text-availability

post_text_availability <- saidit_post_text_check %>%
  summarise(
    total_saidit_posts = n(),
    posts_with_text = sum(
      !is.na(
        possible_content
      )
    ),
    posts_without_text = sum(
      is.na(
        possible_content
      )
    )
  )

post_text_availability
```

If `posts_with_text` is equal to zero, the evidence boundary should be stated clearly:

> The event log confirms the source filename used for the public post, but the full text contents are not preserved in the available data. Therefore, the analysis cannot conclusively interpret the semantic meaning of the post.

```{r}
#| label: audit-suspicious-file-content-availability

suspicious_file_content_audit <- events_enriched %>%
  mutate(
    details_text = map_chr(
      details,
      ~ jsonlite::toJSON(
        .x,
        auto_unbox = TRUE
      )
    )
  ) %>%
  filter(
    str_detect(
      details_text,
      "HiddenOrca|MellowOtter|SwiftWren"
    )
  ) %>%
  mutate(
    possible_saved_content = map_chr(
      details,
      ~ first_non_missing_chr(
        pluck_chr(
          .x,
          "content"
        ),
        pluck_chr(
          .x,
          "text"
        ),
        pluck_chr(
          .x,
          "message"
        ),
        pluck_chr(
          .x,
          "body"
        )
      )
    )
  ) %>%
  select(
    event_order,
    id,
    timestamp_local,
    short_name,
    possible_saved_content
  ) %>%
  filter(
    !is.na(
      possible_saved_content
    )
  )

suspicious_file_content_audit
```

The SaidIT log contains 108 public posts. Among them, 105 posts store embedded text directly in the `content` field. Only three posts do not contain embedded text. These three records instead reference external content-source files: `HiddenOrca.txt`, `MellowOtter.txt`, and `SwiftWren.txt`.

This structural difference distinguishes the suspicious posts from ordinary SaidIT activity. The event log confirms the source filenames, but it does not preserve the full text contents of the three suspicious files.

## 8.9 Extracting Suspicious Pre-Post Checks

The historical evidence suggests that the suspicious SaidIT posts were not released immediately without validation. Each suspicious post was preceded by a `saidit_post_check` event.

However, the pre-post check records do not directly contain the associated suspicious file-family name. Therefore, each suspicious public post is matched to the nearest pre-post check occurring within the previous five seconds.

```{r}
#| label: extract-suspicious-pre-post-checks

saidit_pre_post_checks <- events_enriched %>%
  filter(
    short_name == "saidit_post_check"
  ) %>%
  select(
    check_event_order = event_order,
    check_id = id,
    check_time = timestamp_local,
    check_parties = parties
  )

suspicious_pre_post_checks <- suspicious_saidit_posts %>%
  transmute(
    file_family = content_family,
    post_id = id,
    post_time = timestamp_local
  ) %>%
  inner_join(
    saidit_pre_post_checks,
    by = character(),
    relationship = "many-to-many"
  ) %>%
  mutate(
    seconds_before_post = as.numeric(
      difftime(
        post_time,
        check_time,
        units = "secs"
      )
    )
  ) %>%
  filter(
    seconds_before_post >= 0,
    seconds_before_post <= 5
  ) %>%
  group_by(
    file_family,
    post_id
  ) %>%
  slice_min(
    order_by = seconds_before_post,
    n = 1,
    with_ties = FALSE
  ) %>%
  ungroup() %>%
  arrange(
    post_time
  )

suspicious_pre_post_checks
```

The matching results identify one pre-post check for each suspicious public release. In all three cases, the check occurred exactly one second before publication.

This is an important finding. The validation process was present, but it did not prevent the release of any suspicious post.

## 8.10 Extracting Suspicious File-Deletion Events

The analysis next identifies file-deletion events associated with the three suspicious file families.

Both the `_further_instructions.md` files and the corresponding `.txt` content files are included. These events are relevant because the files were deleted immediately after public release.

```{r}
#| label: extract-suspicious-file-deletions

suspicious_file_deletions <- events_enriched %>%
  filter(
    short_name == "delete_file"
  ) %>%
  mutate(
    deleted_file = coalesce(
      detail_target,
      detail_file,
      file_path
    ),
    file_family = case_when(
      str_detect(
        coalesce(
          deleted_file,
          ""
        ),
        "_further_instructions\\.md$"
      ) ~
        str_remove(
          deleted_file,
          "_further_instructions\\.md$"
        ),
      str_detect(
        coalesce(
          deleted_file,
          ""
        ),
        "\\.txt$"
      ) ~
        str_remove(
          deleted_file,
          "\\.txt$"
        ),
      TRUE ~
        NA_character_
    )
  ) %>%
  filter(
    file_family %in%
      suspicious_file_families
  ) %>%
  select(
    event_order,
    id,
    timestamp_local,
    file_family,
    deleted_file
  ) %>%
  arrange(
    timestamp_local,
    event_order
  )

suspicious_file_deletions
```

The deletion records show that the suspicious instruction files and external content files were removed after publication. This repeated cleanup behaviour reduces the availability of evidence after release and should be treated as part of the incident pattern.

## 8.11 Preparing the Corrected Historical Evidence Matrix

The historical evidence matrix compares whether each suspicious file family contains four stages:

1.  instruction-file handoff;
2.  SaidIT pre-post check;
3.  public SaidIT post;
4.  file deletion.

The matrix is constructed from the corrected event tables rather than relying on direct file-name matching within the pre-post check records.

```{r}
#| label: create-corrected-historical-evidence-matrix

handoff_evidence <- suspicious_handoffs %>%
  distinct(
    file_family
  ) %>%
  transmute(
    file_family,
    event_stage = "Instruction Handoff"
  )

pre_post_check_evidence <- suspicious_pre_post_checks %>%
  distinct(
    file_family
  ) %>%
  transmute(
    file_family,
    event_stage = "Pre-Post Check"
  )

public_post_evidence <- suspicious_saidit_posts %>%
  distinct(
    content_family
  ) %>%
  transmute(
    file_family = content_family,
    event_stage = "Public SaidIT Post"
  )

file_deletion_evidence <- suspicious_file_deletions %>%
  distinct(
    file_family
  ) %>%
  transmute(
    file_family,
    event_stage = "File Deletion"
  )

historical_evidence_matrix <- bind_rows(
  handoff_evidence,
  pre_post_check_evidence,
  public_post_evidence,
  file_deletion_evidence
) %>%
  distinct() %>%
  mutate(
    observed = TRUE
  ) %>%
  complete(
    file_family = suspicious_file_families,
    event_stage = c(
      "Instruction Handoff",
      "Pre-Post Check",
      "Public SaidIT Post",
      "File Deletion"
    ),
    fill = list(
      observed = FALSE
    )
  ) %>%
  pivot_wider(
    names_from = event_stage,
    values_from = observed,
    values_fill = FALSE
  )

historical_evidence_matrix
```

## 8.12 Visualising the Historical Evidence Matrix

The heatmap provides a compact comparison of the three suspicious incidents.

```{r}
#| label: plot-corrected-historical-evidence-matrix
#| fig-width: 9
#| fig-height: 4.8

historical_evidence_matrix_long <- historical_evidence_matrix %>%
  pivot_longer(
    cols = -file_family,
    names_to = "event_stage",
    values_to = "observed"
  ) %>%
  mutate(
    file_family = factor(
      file_family,
      levels = c(
        "HiddenOrca",
        "MellowOtter",
        "SwiftWren"
      )
    ),
    event_stage = factor(
      event_stage,
      levels = c(
        "Instruction Handoff",
        "Pre-Post Check",
        "Public SaidIT Post",
        "File Deletion"
      )
    )
  )

ggplot(
  historical_evidence_matrix_long,
  aes(
    x = event_stage,
    y = file_family,
    fill = observed
  )
) +
  geom_tile(
    color = "white",
    linewidth = 1
  ) +
  geom_text(
    aes(
      label = if_else(
        observed,
        "Observed",
        "Not Observed"
      )
    ),
    size = 4
  ) +
  scale_fill_manual(
    values = c(
      "TRUE" = "#58D68D",
      "FALSE" = "#F5B7B1"
    ),
    guide = "none"
  ) +
  labs(
    title = "Historical Evidence Matrix for Suspicious File Families",
    subtitle = "The matrix compares repeated stages across the three suspicious public-release events",
    x = NULL,
    y = "File Family"
  ) +
  theme_minimal(
    base_size = 13
  ) +
  theme(
    plot.title = element_text(
      face = "bold"
    ),
    axis.text.x = element_text(
      angle = 15,
      hjust = 1
    )
  )
```

The historical evidence matrix reveals the same four-stage pattern across `HiddenOrca`, `MellowOtter`, and `SwiftWren`.

Each incident contains:

1.  repeated instruction-file handoffs;
2.  a SaidIT pre-post check;
3.  a public SaidIT post using an external content-source file; and
4.  immediate deletion of the related files.

The three pre-post checks occurred only one second before public release. However, none of them blocked publication. This indicates that the existing validation mechanism was present but insufficient for detecting high-risk external-file posting behaviour.

## 8.13 Creating the Historical Release Timeline

The following table isolates public-release and file-deletion events across the three suspicious file families.

The timeline is created directly from the suspicious SaidIT-post table and the suspicious file-deletion table.

```{r}
#| label: create-historical-release-timeline

historical_release_timeline <- bind_rows(
  suspicious_saidit_posts %>%
    transmute(
      event_order,
      timestamp_local,
      file_family = content_family,
      event_stage = "Public SaidIT Post",
      event_label = paste0(
        "Published ",
        detail_content_source
      )
    ),
  suspicious_file_deletions %>%
    transmute(
      event_order,
      timestamp_local,
      file_family,
      event_stage = "File Deletion",
      event_label = case_when(
        str_detect(
          deleted_file,
          "_further_instructions\\.md$"
        ) ~
          "Deleted instruction file",
        str_detect(
          deleted_file,
          "\\.txt$"
        ) ~
          "Deleted content file",
        TRUE ~
          paste0(
            "Deleted ",
            deleted_file
          )
      )
    )
) %>%
  mutate(
    file_family = factor(
      file_family,
      levels = c(
        "HiddenOrca",
        "MellowOtter",
        "SwiftWren"
      )
    ),
    event_stage = factor(
      event_stage,
      levels = c(
        "Public SaidIT Post",
        "File Deletion"
      )
    )
  ) %>%
  arrange(
    timestamp_local,
    event_order
  )

historical_release_timeline
```

## 8.14 Visualising Historical Releases and Cleanup Actions

The timeline shows the public release and cleanup stages for each suspicious file family.

```{r}
#| label: plot-historical-release-timeline
#| fig-width: 11
#| fig-height: 5.8

ggplot(
  historical_release_timeline,
  aes(
    x = timestamp_local,
    y = file_family,
    color = event_stage
  )
) +
  geom_point(
    size = 4
  ) +
  geom_label_repel(
    aes(
      label = event_label
    ),
    size = 3.2,
    box.padding = 0.55,
    point.padding = 0.3,
    min.segment.length = 0,
    show.legend = FALSE,
    seed = 1234
  ) +
  scale_color_manual(
    values = c(
      "Public SaidIT Post" = "#E76F51",
      "File Deletion" = "#AF7AC5"
    )
  ) +
  scale_x_datetime(
    date_labels = "%b %d\n%H:%M",
    date_breaks = "1 day",
    expand = expansion(
      mult = c(
        0.05,
        0.1
      )
    )
  ) +
  labs(
    title = "Historical Public Releases and File-Deletion Actions",
    subtitle = "Each suspicious public release is followed by immediate deletion of the related files",
    x = "Local Time",
    y = "File Family",
    color = "Event Stage"
  ) +
  theme_minimal(
    base_size = 13
  ) +
  theme(
    legend.position = "bottom",
    plot.title = element_text(
      face = "bold"
    )
  )
```

The timeline shows that each suspicious public release was followed by deletion of the corresponding instruction file and external content file.

This repeated cleanup pattern is visible across all three file families. The SwiftWren event should therefore be interpreted as the latest occurrence of an unresolved operational failure rather than a completely new incident.

## 8.15 Interactive Historical Release Table

The historical release records are displayed in an interactive table for detailed review.

```{r}
#| label: display-historical-release-timeline

DT::datatable(
  historical_release_timeline,
  rownames = FALSE,
  options = list(
    pageLength = 15,
    scrollX = TRUE
  )
)
```

::: callout-warning
## Historical Pattern Evidence

The latest SwiftWren event was not an isolated malfunction.

The historical analysis identifies the same four-stage operational pattern across `HiddenOrca`, `MellowOtter`, and `SwiftWren`:

`instruction-file propagation → pre-post check → public release → immediate file deletion`

The logs establish a recurring agent-system failure pathway. However, they do not establish whether the behaviour was intentionally malicious or caused by unsafe automated workflow design.
:::

# 9 Recommended Intervention and Preventive Control Design

## 9.1 Selecting One Intervention Point

The historical analysis identifies a recurring four-stage pattern:

1.  suspicious instruction-file handoffs;
2.  a SaidIT pre-post check;
3.  a public SaidIT post using an external content-source file; and
4.  immediate deletion of the related files.

The three suspicious posts all passed through an existing SaidIT pre-post check exactly one second before public release. However, the current check did not prevent publication.

This report recommends strengthening one intervention point:

> **The SaidIT pre-post validation gate immediately before public release.**

This location is selected because every public SaidIT post must pass through the same gateway. Upstream agent-to-agent handoffs may follow different routes, involve different actors, or change over time. In contrast, the final posting gateway is a consistent control point.

## 9.2 Defining the Proposed Validation Logic

The proposed control uses a transparent rule-based design.

A SaidIT post should be routed to human review when it uses an external content-source file rather than embedded text. The validation gate should also record whether the external file belongs to a family associated with instruction-file propagation.

The initial rule is deliberately simple:

``` text
If a SaidIT post uses an external content-source file:
    Block direct publication
    Route the post to human review
Otherwise:
    Allow the standard publication workflow
```

This rule is explainable and directly supported by the historical evidence. Among the 108 SaidIT posts, 105 contain embedded text. Only three use external content-source files. These three records correspond exactly to `HiddenOrca.txt`, `MellowOtter.txt`, and `SwiftWren.txt`.

## 9.3 Creating a Post-Level Validation Dataset

The SaidIT posting events are converted into a validation table.

The table records:

- whether embedded text is available;
- whether an external content-source file is used;
- the associated file family;
- whether the file family is linked to suspicious instruction-file propagation;
- whether the proposed control would route the post to human review.

```{r}
#| label: create-post-validation-dataset

saidit_validation_dataset <- events_enriched %>%
  filter(
    short_name == "saidit_post"
  ) %>%
  mutate(
    parties_display = map_chr(
      parties,
      ~ paste(
        .x,
        collapse = ", "
      )
    ),
    embedded_text = map_chr(
      details,
      ~ first_non_missing_chr(
        pluck_chr(
          .x,
          "content"
        ),
        pluck_chr(
          .x,
          "text"
        ),
        pluck_chr(
          .x,
          "message"
        ),
        pluck_chr(
          .x,
          "body"
        ),
        pluck_chr(
          .x,
          "post"
        )
      )
    ),
    external_content_source = !is.na(
      detail_content_source
    ),
    content_family = str_remove(
      detail_content_source,
      "\\.txt$"
    ),
    linked_instruction_chain = content_family %in%
      suspicious_file_families,
    proposed_action = if_else(
      external_content_source,
      "Route to Human Review",
      "Allow Standard Workflow"
    )
  ) %>%
  select(
    event_order,
    id,
    timestamp_local,
    parties_display,
    embedded_text,
    detail_content_source,
    content_family,
    external_content_source,
    linked_instruction_chain,
    proposed_action
  ) %>%
  arrange(
    timestamp_local,
    event_order
  )

saidit_validation_dataset
```

## 9.4 Summarising the Proposed Validation Rule

The summary table compares posts that would remain in the standard workflow with posts that would be routed to human review.

```{r}
#| label: summarise-proposed-validation-rule

validation_rule_summary <- saidit_validation_dataset %>%
  count(
    proposed_action,
    name = "number_of_posts"
  ) %>%
  mutate(
    share_of_posts = number_of_posts / sum(
      number_of_posts
    )
  )

validation_rule_summary
```

## 9.5 Visualising the Validation Outcome

```{r}
#| label: plot-validation-rule-summary
#| fig-width: 8.5
#| fig-height: 5

ggplot(
  validation_rule_summary,
  aes(
    x = proposed_action,
    y = number_of_posts,
    fill = proposed_action
  )
) +
  geom_col(
    width = 0.62,
    show.legend = FALSE
  ) +
  geom_text(
    aes(
      label = paste0(
        number_of_posts,
        " posts\n",
        percent(
          share_of_posts,
          accuracy = 0.1
        )
      )
    ),
    vjust = -0.35,
    size = 4
  ) +
  scale_fill_manual(
    values = c(
      "Allow Standard Workflow" = "#5DADE2",
      "Route to Human Review" = "#E76F51"
    )
  ) +
  scale_y_continuous(
    expand = expansion(
      mult = c(
        0,
        0.15
      )
    )
  ) +
  labs(
    title = "Impact of the Proposed SaidIT Validation Gate",
    subtitle = "Only posts using external content-source files are routed to human review",
    x = NULL,
    y = "Number of SaidIT Posts"
  ) +
  theme_minimal(
    base_size = 13
  ) +
  theme(
    plot.title = element_text(
      face = "bold"
    ),
    axis.text.x = element_text(
      face = "bold"
    )
  )
```

The proposed validation gate preserves the standard workflow for 105 of the 108 SaidIT posts. Only three posts are routed to human review, resulting in an overall review rate of 2.8%. This indicates that the rule can improve safety without creating a substantial operational burden.

## 9.6 Reviewing Posts Routed to Human Review

The following table shows which historical posts would have been intercepted by the proposed control.

```{r}
#| label: review-flagged-saidit-posts

flagged_saidit_posts <- saidit_validation_dataset %>%
  filter(
    proposed_action == "Route to Human Review"
  ) %>%
  select(
    timestamp_local,
    parties_display,
    detail_content_source,
    content_family,
    external_content_source,
    linked_instruction_chain,
    proposed_action
  )

flagged_saidit_posts
```

## 9.7 Interactive Flagged-Post Table

```{r}
#| label: display-flagged-saidit-posts

DT::datatable(
  flagged_saidit_posts,
  rownames = FALSE,
  options = list(
    dom = "t",
    scrollX = TRUE
  )
)
```

The proposed control routes the three historically suspicious external-file posts to human review:

- `HiddenOrca.txt`
- `MellowOtter.txt`
- `SwiftWren.txt`

## 9.8 Evaluating Detection Coverage

The proposed rule is evaluated against the three historically suspicious posts.

```{r}
#| label: evaluate-validation-coverage

validation_coverage_summary <- saidit_validation_dataset %>%
  summarise(
    total_saidit_posts = n(),
    posts_allowed_standard_workflow = sum(
      proposed_action == "Allow Standard Workflow"
    ),
    posts_routed_to_review = sum(
      proposed_action == "Route to Human Review"
    ),
    suspicious_posts_observed = sum(
      linked_instruction_chain
    ),
    suspicious_posts_routed_to_review = sum(
      linked_instruction_chain &
        proposed_action == "Route to Human Review"
    ),
    historical_detection_rate = suspicious_posts_routed_to_review /
      suspicious_posts_observed,
    review_rate_for_all_posts = posts_routed_to_review /
      total_saidit_posts
  )

validation_coverage_summary
```

## 9.9 Displaying the Validation Coverage Metrics

```{r}
#| label: display-validation-coverage-metrics

validation_coverage_display <- validation_coverage_summary %>%
  transmute(
    `Total SaidIT Posts` = total_saidit_posts,
    `Posts Allowed Standard Workflow` = posts_allowed_standard_workflow,
    `Posts Routed to Human Review` = posts_routed_to_review,
    `Historically Suspicious Posts` = suspicious_posts_observed,
    `Suspicious Posts Routed to Review` = suspicious_posts_routed_to_review,
    `Historical Detection Rate` = percent(
      historical_detection_rate,
      accuracy = 0.1
    ),
    `Overall Review Rate` = percent(
      review_rate_for_all_posts,
      accuracy = 0.1
    )
  )

validation_coverage_display
```

When applied retrospectively, the proposed rule identifies all three historically suspicious posts. The historical detection rate is 100%, while the overall review rate is only 2.8%. This result supports the use of an external-file check as a targeted first-line control.

## 9.10 Proposed Control Workflow

The proposed control strengthens the existing SaidIT pre-post check.

The improved validation process is shown below.

```{r}
#| label: display-proposed-control-workflow

DiagrammeR::grViz(
  "
  digraph proposed_control_workflow {

    graph [
      layout = dot,
      rankdir = LR,
      bgcolor = transparent
    ]

    node [
      shape = box,
      style = 'rounded,filled',
      fontname = Helvetica,
      fontsize = 12,
      margin = 0.15
    ]

    edge [
      fontname = Helvetica,
      fontsize = 11,
      color = '#7F8C8D',
      arrowsize = 0.8
    ]

    A [
      label = 'Agent-Generated\\nSaidIT Post',
      fillcolor = '#D6EAF8',
      color = '#5DADE2'
    ]

    B [
      label = 'Existing Pre-Post\\nValidation Gate',
      fillcolor = '#FCF3CF',
      color = '#F5B041'
    ]

    C [
      label = 'External Content-\\nSource File?',
      shape = diamond,
      fillcolor = '#FDEBD0',
      color = '#EB984E'
    ]

    D [
      label = 'Allow Standard\\nWorkflow',
      fillcolor = '#D5F5E3',
      color = '#58D68D'
    ]

    E [
      label = 'Block Direct\\nPublication',
      fillcolor = '#FADBD8',
      color = '#E76F51'
    ]

    F [
      label = 'Trace Linked\\nInstruction File',
      fillcolor = '#E8DAEF',
      color = '#AF7AC5'
    ]

    G [
      label = 'Route to\\nHuman Review',
      fillcolor = '#FADBD8',
      color = '#E76F51'
    ]

    H [
      label = 'Reviewer\\nApproval?',
      shape = diamond,
      fillcolor = '#FCF3CF',
      color = '#F5B041'
    ]

    I [
      label = 'Publish with\\nAudit Record',
      fillcolor = '#D5F5E3',
      color = '#58D68D'
    ]

    J [
      label = 'Quarantine File and\\nPreserve Evidence',
      fillcolor = '#F5B7B1',
      color = '#CB4335'
    ]

    A -> B
    B -> C

    C -> D [
      label = 'No'
    ]

    C -> E [
      label = 'Yes'
    ]

    E -> F
    F -> G
    G -> H

    H -> I [
      label = 'Approved'
    ]

    H -> J [
      label = 'Rejected'
    ]
  }
  "
)
```

## 9.11 Why This Intervention Point Is Effective

The proposed pre-post validation gate is selected for three reasons.

### Consistent control location

Agent-to-agent propagation paths are complex and may change over time. The `SwiftWren` chain alone contains 186 handoffs involving 18 agents. A control placed at one upstream agent would not reliably block future variants.

In contrast, all public SaidIT posts must pass through the same posting gateway.

### Strong historical signal

The event log contains 108 SaidIT posts. Among them, 105 store embedded text directly. Only three posts use external content-source files. These three records correspond exactly to the suspicious `HiddenOrca`, `MellowOtter`, and `SwiftWren` incidents.

### Limited operational burden

The proposed rule routes only external-file posts to human review. Standard posts with embedded text continue through the existing workflow.

This design improves safety without requiring manual review of every ordinary SaidIT post.

## 9.12 Limitations of the Proposed Rule

The rule is designed as an interpretable first-line control rather than a complete cybersecurity solution.

Several limitations remain:

- an attacker or unsafe agent may embed suspicious content directly rather than using an external file;
- external files may sometimes be legitimate;
- a human-review process requires operational ownership and response-time standards;
- file contents should be preserved for audit rather than deleted immediately after a blocked release;
- additional controls may be needed upstream to reduce repeated propagation.

The proposed control should therefore be treated as a targeted intervention at the most reliable release gateway, not as a replacement for broader system monitoring.

::: callout-warning
## Recommended Intervention

The recommended intervention is a strengthened SaidIT pre-post validation gate.

Any agent-generated public post that uses an external content-source file should be blocked from direct publication and routed to human review.

This rule is supported by the historical evidence:

- 108 SaidIT posts are observed;
- 105 posts store embedded text directly;
- only 3 posts reference external content-source files;
- those 3 posts correspond exactly to `HiddenOrca`, `MellowOtter`, and `SwiftWren`;
- all 3 suspicious posts passed through the existing pre-post check without being blocked.

The proposed rule would have intercepted the three historical suspicious releases while leaving the standard workflow unchanged for most ordinary posts.
:::

# 10 Key Findings

## 10.1 Summary Metrics

The investigation combines event-level, actor-level, network-level, and content-source evidence.

The following table summarises the most important analytical results.

```{r}
#| label: create-executive-summary-metrics

executive_summary_metrics <- tibble(
  metric = c(
    "Total Internal Events Analysed",
    "Organization Nodes",
    "Organization Edges",
    "Suspicious Instruction-File Families",
    "Suspicious Agent-to-Agent Handoffs",
    "SwiftWren Handoffs",
    "SwiftWren Unique Agents",
    "Suspicious SaidIT Posts",
    "Total SaidIT Posts",
    "Posts Routed to Human Review",
    "Historical Detection Rate",
    "Overall Review Rate"
  ),
  value = c(
    comma(
      nrow(
        events_clean
      )
    ),
    comma(
      nrow(
        org_nodes
      )
    ),
    comma(
      nrow(
        org_edges
      )
    ),
    comma(
      n_distinct(
        suspicious_handoffs$file_family
      )
    ),
    comma(
      nrow(
        suspicious_handoffs
      )
    ),
    comma(
      sum(
        suspicious_handoffs$file_family == "SwiftWren"
      )
    ),
    comma(
      suspicious_chain_summary %>%
        filter(
          file_family == "SwiftWren"
        ) %>%
        pull(
          unique_agents
        )
    ),
    comma(
      nrow(
        suspicious_saidit_posts
      )
    ),
    comma(
      nrow(
        saidit_validation_dataset
      )
    ),
    comma(
      sum(
        saidit_validation_dataset$proposed_action ==
          "Route to Human Review"
      )
    ),
    percent(
      validation_coverage_summary$historical_detection_rate,
      accuracy = 0.1
    ),
    percent(
      validation_coverage_summary$review_rate_for_all_posts,
      accuracy = 0.1
    )
  )
)

executive_summary_metrics
```

